00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.530
Leah: We can see by looking at the
different languages that can be learnt

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.090
that programming languages can be
used for the various functions that

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.179
computers and devices can perform.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.089
Let's look at some of the terms that
are used in relation to different

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:14.879
types of language and programming.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:17.049
Jack: Programming Paradigm

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:21.509
A programming paradigm doesn't
refer to a specific language

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.630
but rather to a way to program.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.529
Some languages make it easy to write
in some paradigms, but not others.

00:00:30.139 --> 00:00:31.489
Leah: Functional Programming

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.920
Functional programming languages
are based on mathematical functions

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.040
that use linear mathematical logic.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.839
They work well with list processing
applications and large data analysis.

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:45.849
Jack: Object Oriented

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.650
The most popular programming languages
in the current arena as we have

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.119
seen, are known as 'object oriented'.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.570
This means that the language
is organised around groupings

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.210
of data also called 'objects'.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.880
These types of languages are therefore
often easy to manipulate, reuse and

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.770
scale, making them quite versatile
and ideal for structuring software.

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:11.029
So, let's look at the differences
between object oriented languages and

00:01:11.029 --> 00:01:14.860
procedural programming and the reasons
most popular modern languages have

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.230
adopted the Object Oriented style.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.670
Leah: Procedural programming

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.049
In procedural programming, a program is
divided into small parts also known as

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:27.709
'functions' [routines and sub routines].

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.420
This is often referred to as inline
programming taking a form of top-down

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.490
approach, solving problems from the top
of the code to the bottom, through a

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.240
series of steps that must be carried out.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.929
These procedures are broken down through
'functional decomposition' until a sub

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.069
procedure is simple enough to be solved.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.710
If, however, edits are needed, then
every line of code associated to the

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.840
original change needs to be edited too.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.630
This makes it harder to locate all the
related elements that need to be altered.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.720
Procedural programming does not
also, have a proper method for

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.859
hiding data so it is less secure.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.220
Adding data and function is not
easy and the function is often more

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.980
important than the data itself.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.950
Examples of languages used in
procedural programming are FORTRAN,

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.390
ALGOL, COBOL, BASIC, Pascal and C.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.010
Jack: Object Oriented Programming

00:02:22.389 --> 00:02:25.600
Object Oriented Programming
is a programming model based

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.280
around the principle of objects.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.679
It uses collections of objects to pass
on messages when called upon, to request

00:02:32.679 --> 00:02:34.559
specific services or information.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.680
Objects contain data in the
form of attributes and code.

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:43.570
Object oriented programming has
access specifiers such as private,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.970
public, protected and this ability
to hide data means it is more secure.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.270
Adding data is easier as new objects can
inherit characteristics from existing ones

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.549
making adjustment of programs simpler.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.079
Data is more important than the
function and it is based more

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.640
on the real-world environment.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.880
This form of programming
is a bottom-up approach.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.090
Many object-oriented programming
languages will dump or destroy

00:03:09.139 --> 00:03:13.070
unused objects, freeing up system
memory which enables systems to

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.839
run faster and more effectively.

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:18.759
Examples of languages used in
object-oriented programming

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:26.730
are Java, JavaScript, C++,
C#, Python, PHP, Ruby, etc.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.220
As you can see by the list of
languages, we have looked at already

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.710
they all fall into the category
of object oriented languages.

